The C3 pathway is the most common and ancient form, where carbon dioxide is fixed directly into a 3-carbon compound; it is efficient in cool, wet climates but suffers from wasteful photorespiration in hot, dry conditions. C4 plants evolved a two-step system that first captures CO₂ into a 4-carbon molecule in mesophyll cells, then shuttles it to bundle sheath cells for the Calvin cycle—this concentrates CO₂ and minimizes photorespiration, making C4 ideal for hot, sunny environments. CAM plants, typical of succulents like cacti, open their stomata at night to take in CO₂ and store it as a 4-carbon acid, then release it during the day for photosynthesis, allowing them to conserve water in arid conditions.
