The TCP three-way handshake is the process used to establish a reliable connection between a client and a server before data transmission begins. It works in three steps: first, the client sends a SYN (synchronize) packet to the server to initiate the connection. Second, the server responds with a SYN-ACK (synchronize-acknowledgment) packet, acknowledging the client's request and sending its own synchronization signal. Third, the client replies with an ACK (acknowledgment) packet, confirming the connection is established. After this exchange, both sides know the connection is ready for reliable, two-way data transfer.
