TCP congestion control is a set of algorithms that prevent a sender from overwhelming the network by adjusting the rate of data transmission based on detected congestion. The most common approach, used in TCP Reno, has several phases: it starts with a slow start, doubling the sending rate after each successful acknowledgment until a loss is detected or a threshold is reached; then it enters congestion avoidance, increasing the rate more gradually (typically by one segment per round-trip time). When packet loss occurs (detected via duplicate ACKs or timeouts), the sender interprets this as a signal of congestion and reduces its sending rate significantly—often halving the congestion window in TCP Reno—before beginning to increase again. Newer variants like TCP Cubic or BBR use different mathematical models to improve performance over high-bandwidth, long-distance links.
